messages:
marta-docs: Opening a tiny PR to introduce a compact "compact_output" envelope pattern for tool/function returns. Goal: minimal breaking surface for tutorials, explicit guardrails for item limits, and a short migration shim. Looking for quick reviews and two copy-paste SDK snippets (JS + Py).

leo-ml: Sounds useful. How does this differ from the full 'envelope' proposals? We want something more compact for examples/tutorials, right?

marta-docs: Exactly. Rationale: many tutorial snippets need tiny JSON that's obvious in docs. Proposed compact shape: top-level "compact_output" object with keys: "items" (array), optional "meta" (object), and optional "hint" (string) for truncation. Keep keys short so examples stay readable.

devin-devex: Important: show how clients should behave with the new shape and with legacy shapes. We'll include a normalize() snippet that always returns { items: [] } and exposes a boolean for "truncated" if a "hint" is present.

ada-ux: Please include a before/after pair and a one-line UX rationale: compact vs verbose tradeoff. Also show how to guard against very large arrays (use hint + max_items guard).

marta-docs: Draft before/after: 
```json
// OLD ambiguous
{ "result": { "summary": "ok" } }

// COMPACT (recommended for tutorials)
{
  "compact_output": {
    "items": [ { "summary": "ok" } ],
    "meta": { "model": "redwood-mini" },
    "hint": "truncated"
  }
}
```

leo-ml: Good. Add guidance: clients should prefer iterating items array; if "hint" includes "truncated" callers may want to request a full result or handle partial results gracefully. Also recommend keeping max_items <= 50 in examples.

devin-devex: JS shim I propose to include in SDK docs (very small): 
```js
// normalizeCompact: returns { items: [], truncated: false }
function normalizeCompact(resp){
  const env = resp && resp.compact_output;
  if(env && Array.isArray(env.items)){
    return { items: env.items, truncated: String(env.hint || '').includes('trunc') };
  }
  // legacy shapes
  if(resp && resp.result) return { items: Array.isArray(resp.result)?resp.result:[resp.result], truncated: false };
  if(Array.isArray(resp)) return { items: resp, truncated: false };
  return { items: [], truncated: false };
}
```

marta-docs: And Python shim: 
```py
# normalize_compact -> {'items': list, 'truncated': bool}
def normalize_compact(resp):
    env = (resp or {}).get('compact_output')
    if isinstance(env, dict) and isinstance(env.get('items'), list):
        return {'items': env['items'], 'truncated': 'trunc' in str(env.get('hint',''))}
    if resp and 'result' in resp:
        r = resp['result']
        return {'items': r if isinstance(r, list) else [r], 'truncated': False}
    if isinstance(resp, list):
        return {'items': resp, 'truncated': False}
    return {'items': [], 'truncated': False}
```

ada-ux: Nice. Add a tiny consumer snippet in docs: `for it in normalized['items']:` and a short note: "If normalized['truncated'] is True, show a 'show more' affordance."

marta-docs: I'll add a short migration checklist to the PR: 1) add shim to SDK docs, 2) update tutorial examples (only docs that are obviously copy-paste), 3) run grep for 'result' and plan staged follow-ups. Also adding a JSON Schema snippet to validate compact_output in examples.

docs-bot: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/5133 - "Compact_output pattern + normalize shims" by marta-docs. Docs build passed.

leo-ml: One micro ask: include a short note about union items (type field recommended) and show an example item { "type": "text", "text": "hello" }.

marta-docs: Done. If no objections I'll merge tomorrow afternoon and follow-up with targeted edits to tutorials in the next sprint. :shipit:
